pumac-2020-team-13.body:
Suppose Will's first $n$ values $a_1,\dots,a_n$ are consistent with some integer polynomial. Any two integer polynomials $p,q$ with $p(i)=q(i)=a_i$ for $i\le n$ differ by $(x-1)(x-2)\cdots(x-n)\cdot r(x)$ with $r$ an integer polynomial, so $p(n+1)\equiv q(n+1)\pmod{n!}$. Hence $a_{n+1}$ must lie in one specific residue class mod $n!$ (and some value in that class works). Will's random value is uniform on $[1,(n+2)!]$, a multiple of $n!$ in length, so he passes round $n+1$ with probability exactly $\frac1{n!}$.

Round $1$ and round $2$ cost nothing ($\frac1{0!}$ and $\frac1{1!}$: any two values lie on a line). Therefore the probability of getting through round $n$ is $\prod_{i=1}^{n-1}\frac1{i!}$, and for $n=20$:
$$\frac ab=\prod_{i=1}^{19}\frac1{i!},\qquad b=\prod_{i=1}^{19}i!\ \text{(in lowest terms)}.$$
We need the total number of prime factors of $b$ counted with multiplicity: for each prime $p$, $\sum_{i=1}^{19}\sum_{k\ge1}\lfloor i/p^k\rfloor$:
- $p=19,17,13,11$: $20-p$ each: $1+3+7+9=20$;
- $p=7$: $7\cdot1+6\cdot2=19$;
- $p=5$: $5\cdot1+5\cdot2+5\cdot3=30$;
- $p=3$: $(3(1+2+3+4+5)+2\cdot6)+(9\cdot1+2\cdot2)=45+25=70$;
- $p=2$: $2(1+\dots+9)+4(1+2+3+4)+8\cdot1+2\cdot4+4\cdot1=90+40+8+8+4=150$.
Total: $20+19+30+70+150=289$.

**Answer: 289**
